#!/usr/bin/env python3
# version: 8
"""herdr-push: tells your phone when a herdr agent or a work board task needs you.

It runs on the machine with herdr, watches its agents (`herdr api snapshot`) and, where the board
is installed, its tasks (`herdr-projects list --json`), and sends a push to every phone that
subscribed (Herdr for Android sets this up over SSH) when:

  - an agent becomes blocked (it asks something) or finishes, and when that is over again;
  - a board task starts waiting for you (ready to approve, review failed, plan ready, pick one,
    asks you, stopped, went wrong), and when it no longer does;
  - for a phone that asked for Live Updates: which agents and board tasks are running, with their
    stage and progress, as that changes (a few times a minute at most);
  - the machine runs low on RAM (under 10% available) or on a disk, naming what uses the most, and
    when it has room again. It also keeps the last hour of RAM and CPU readings for the app's chart.
  - an AI quick command that the app started there ends (done, failed, or at its limit), with the
    start of its answer;
  - an AI provider the machine uses passes 80% or 95% of a limit, or runs out (Claude Code's and
    Codex's 5-hour and weekly limits, an API key's limit, as herdr-usage reads them where the app put
    it), and when the limit is reset; and when the board's fallback takes a task over from one agent
    to another because of a limit ("codex took over from claude: out of allowance until 17:00");
  - a task or an agent a phone watches ("tell me when API-3's checks pass") changes, for that phone.

Each push says whether someone is at this machine: how long ago someone typed in a herdr client here
(its terminal's last input, as `w` reads it, or the presence file being touched), and whether a
desktop session here is unlocked. A phone can then keep quiet while you sit at the machine.

It also keeps a journal of the last week: each time a board task is done, starts waiting for you,
goes wrong, starts or is added, and each time an agent finishes or asks something. The app reads it
(`herdr-push journal`) for its brief of what happened while you weren't looking.

A push goes to the phone's UnifiedPush address (a push app such as ntfy gives it), sealed with a key
only that phone and this machine know: the push server reads nothing, and the phone takes no push
that wasn't sealed with it. Python 3 alone, no packages.

  herdr-push run                 watch and send (what the service runs)
  herdr-push subscribe           add or replace a phone, its JSON on stdin
  herdr-push unsubscribe <id>    remove a phone
  herdr-push test [<id>]         send a test notification now
  herdr-push status              how it stands, as JSON
  herdr-push ensure              start the watcher when it isn't running
  herdr-push install             start it now and with the machine (systemd, launchd or cron)
  herdr-push uninstall [--purge] stop it and remove the start-up entry (--purge: and its settings)
  herdr-push once [--dry-run]    one look, printing what it would send
  herdr-push journal [--session NAME] [--since SECONDS]
                                 what happened, as JSON (the app's brief reads it)
  herdr-push presence            whether someone is at this machine, as JSON
  herdr-push pair [--host NAME[,NAME…]] [--port N] [--session NAME] [--name NAME]
                                 show a QR code a phone scans to add this machine (one-time, 5 minutes)

See host/herdr-push/README.md in the herdr-android repo.
"""

